`db::DB_WRITE_LOCK.lock().unwrap_or_else(|e| e.into_inner())` is the codebase's established convention, BUT the convention is paired with a diagnostic `eprintln!("[mutex_poisoned] db::DB_WRITE_LOCK: {e:?}")` per C3-1 / EVNB-02 (see `src-tauri/src/db/mod.rs:333-334`, `src-tauri/src/db/bot_history.rs:92-93`, `src-tauri/src/bot_chat.rs:1327-1328`, etc. — all 10+ sites follow this pattern). This test's `cleanup` and the conflict-target insertion block (line ~268) both omit the eprintln and silently recover from poisoning. If `DB_WRITE_LOCK` ever becomes poisoned during this test's lifecycle, subsequent assertions will run against potentially inconsistent state (e.g., half-applied applied.jsonl, partial lesson write) without any diagnostic trail in stderr. Add the eprintln to match the convention:
```rust
let _db = db::DB_WRITE_LOCK.lock().unwrap_or_else(|e| {
    eprintln!("[mutex_poisoned] db::DB_WRITE_LOCK (evolution_gov cleanup/insert): {e:?}");
    e.into_inner()
});
```
This test is especially important to surface poisoning because it is the one that exercises the human-approval executor and the anti-hijack gate — exactly the paths where a half-applied state would matter.

